Prefer H.264 High profile, and make the encoded colour range selectable.

Two things the sink cannot be trusted to negotiate, so neither is left to it --
but neither is hardcoded either, because the right answer differs per sink.

Sinks routinely advertise WFD_H264_PROFILE_BASE only (this Samsung reports
profile 1) while their decoder handles High perfectly well for ordinary video.
constrained-baseline forbids CABAC and the 8x8 transform, and vah264enc defaults
both to TRUE, so the caps were switching them off at a cost of roughly 15-25%
efficiency -- at 2560x1600@60 that is ~0.13 bits/pixel, where the loss shows.
High is therefore the default; GND_FORCE_PROFILE=base accommodates a sink that
genuinely cannot decode it without needing a rebuild.

Nothing set colorimetry anywhere, so the RGB->YUV conversion produced BT.709
limited range (16-235) and vah264enc -- which has no colour properties of its own
and inherits its input caps -- signalled that. Limited range is spec-correct and
stays the default, but sinks that assume full range for a PC-style resolution
render 16 as dark grey instead of black, i.e. washed out; those want
GND_COLOR_RANGE=full. The filter has to sit after videoconvert: wfd-sinkfilter is
upstream of it and fed by videoscale, which cannot convert colour at all, so
constraining there makes the pipeline unnegotiable ("not-negotiated (-4)" out of
screencastsrc).

Not upstreamed.

